  "summary": "EFF's 2025 year-in-review recaps its investigations into Flock Safety's ALPR network, documenting surveillance of protesters, discriminatory searches targeting Romani people, use in a Texas abortion-related investigation, and expansion into audio detection. It highlights resulting federal and state investigations, an ACLU/EFF lawsuit against San Jose, and municipalities (Austin, Evanston, Eugene) canceling or declining to renew Flock contracts.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "A Johnson County official ran two searches with the note \"had an abortion, search for female.\" The second search probed 6,809 networks, accessing 83,345 cameras across nearly the entire country.",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "more than 80 law enforcement agencies used language perpetuating harmful stereotypes against Romani people when searching the nationwide Flock Safety ALPR network",
      "paragraph_idx": 8
    },
    {
      "quote": "Communities from Austin to Evanston to Eugene successfully canceled or refused to renew their Flock contracts",
      "paragraph_idx": 17
    },
    {
      "quote": "SJPD and other California law enforcement agencies searched San Jose's database 3,965,519 times",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    }
  ],
